            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\n\nI indeed can reproduce. The problem is that the response from the LSP server (for some reason) uses `vim.NIL` to indicate missing fields. Here is one item from the `'textoDocument/completion'` response:\n\n```lua\n{\n  client_id = 1,\n  detail = \"\",\n  documentation = vim.NIL,\n  insertText = vim.NIL,\n  insertTextFormat = vim.NIL,\n  kind = 14,\n  label = \"if\"\n}\n```\n\nThis is visible here because `insertText` is registered as present which by LSP specification means that it must be string. So it is used to compute completion candidate string.\n\nAlthough this is possible to resolve in 'mini.completion', at the moment I don't think it is its fault. The returned result does not follow the LSP specification, so the problem is either Neovim's `vim.lsp.buf_request_all()` or LSP server. My guess is the first, but that would need a further investigation.\n\nSolving this in 'mini.completion' by explicitly checking for `vim.NIL` each time there is a check for `nil` would also add non-trivial amount of performance overhead. So I think the best course of action here is:\n\n- Localize the issue and report upstream (Neovim or `bashd`).\n- Tweak only the `bashd` behavior locally to ensure all `vim.NIL` are converted to `nil`. Like by creating `~/.config/nvim/after/ftplugin/sh.lua` with the following content:\n\n    ```lua\n    local custom_process_items = function(items, base)\n      local res = MiniCompletion.default_process_items(items, base)\n      for i = 1, #res do\n        for k, v in pairs(res[i]) do\n          res[i][k] = v ~= vim.NIL and v or nil\n        end\n      end\n      return res\n    end\n    vim.b.minicompletion_config = { lsp_completion = { process_items = custom_process_items } }\n    ```",
